\begin{proof}
Let $L=\mathbb R\times\mathbb R$ with the dictionary order. The dictionary order topology is the order topology of the lexicographic sum of copies of the ordered space $\mathbb R$, indexed by the ordered space $\mathbb R$.

We apply the lexicographic-sum metrization theorem: a lexicographic sum of metrizable linear orders is metrizable when the index order and the fibers have countable cofinality and coinitiality at every point. Both copies of $\mathbb R$ are metrizable linear orders, and every real number has countable cofinality and coinitiality, since rational sequences may approach it from below and above. Hence the lexicographic sum is metrizable. Therefore $\mathbb R\times\mathbb R$ in the dictionary order topology is metrizable.
\end{proof}
